Now let me analyze the settle logic carefully:

```dart
Future<String> liveWaitForScreen(
    Pattern pattern, {
    Duration timeout = const Duration(seconds: 10),
    bool settle = false,
  }) => _live(() async {
    final deadline = DateTime.now().add(timeout);
    while (DateTime.now().isBefore(deadline)) {
      final screen = screenText;
      if (screen.contains(pattern)) {
        if (!settle) return screen;
        // Frame-completion gate: hold until the byte stream has been quiet
        // for two settle turns (the frame is fully applied) — bounded by a
        // grace so a still-streaming screen (spinner ticks) cannot hang the
        // wait past legacy semantics. If the pattern vanished while
        // settling (a transient state), keep polling for a stable match.
        await waitForRawSettle(
          settleMs: 200,
          grace: const Duration(seconds: 2),
        );
        final settled = screenText;
        if (settled.contains(pattern)) return settled;
      }
      await Future<void>.delayed(const Duration(milliseconds: 50));
    }
    throw TimeoutException(...);
  });
```

Potential issue: `waitForRawSettle` can take up to the full `grace` (2s) EVERY polling iteration. If the pattern appears on screen but the raw stream keeps streaming (spinner), `waitForRawSettle` returns false after 2s, `settled` might not contain the pattern → loop continues → another 2s `waitForRawSettle`. With a 15s timeout, that's fine but each iteration burns 2s+. Consider: if the pattern vanishes while settling (a transient state), keep polling. Worst case: match appears at t = timeout - 0.1s, then settle runs 2s past the deadline. The deadline check `DateTime.now().isBefore(deadline)` is only checked at the top of the loop, so total runtime can overshoot the timeout by up to ~2s + 50ms. Minor, bounded. Not a bug, just a nit — worth mentioning maybe as a suggestion.

Another subtle issue in `waitForRawSettle`:

```dart
var lastLength = -1;
var stableTurns = 0;
while (DateTime.now().isBefore(deadline)) {
  await Future<void>.delayed(Duration(milliseconds: settleMs));
  if (_rawBuffer.length == lastLength) {
    if (++stableTurns >= 2) return true;
  } else {
    stableTurns = 0;
    lastLength = _rawBuffer.length;
  }
return false;
```

First iteration: lastLength = -1, rawBuffer.length >= 0 always != -1, so stableTurns = 0, lastLength = current length. So effectively it needs 3 delays (~600ms min) to return true: first iteration always sets lastLength, then two stable turns. Same as `waitForOutput` logic — consistent with existing code ("Same stability rule as [waitForOutput]"). Duplicated logic — waitForOutput has the exact same loop. The new `waitForRawSettle` duplicates `waitForOutput`'s settle loop but returns bool instead of the buffer. Could have been implemented by extracting a shared helper or by having waitForRawSettle call a common private method. That's a duplication SUGGESTION.

