var stableTurns = 0;
while (DateTime.now().isBefore(deadline)) {
  await Future<void>.delayed(Duration(milliseconds: settleMs));
  if (_rawBuffer.length == lastLength) {
    if (++stableTurns >= 2) return true;
  } else {
    stableTurns = 0;
    lastLength = _rawBuffer.length;
  }
return false;
```

First iteration: lastLength = -1, rawBuffer.length >= 0 always != -1, so stableTurns = 0, lastLength = current length. So effectively it needs 3 delays (~600ms min) to return true: first iteration always sets lastLength, then two stable turns. Same as `waitForOutput` logic — consistent with existing code ("Same stability rule as [waitForOutput]"). Duplicated logic — waitForOutput has the exact same loop. The new `waitForRawSettle` duplicates `waitForOutput`'s settle loop but returns bool instead of the buffer. Could have been implemented by extracting a shared helper or by having waitForRawSettle call a common private method. That's a duplication SUGGESTION.

