```dart
Future<bool> waitForRawSettle({
  int settleMs = 200,
  Duration grace = const Duration(seconds: 2),
}) async {
  final deadline = DateTime.now().add(grace);
  var lastLength = -1;
  var stableTurns = 0;
  while (DateTime.now().isBefore(deadline)) {
    await Future<void>.delayed(Duration(milliseconds: settleMs));
    if (_rawBuffer.length == lastLength) {
      if (++stableTurns >= 2) return true;
    } else {
      stableTurns = 0;
      lastLength = _rawBuffer.length;
    }
  }
  return false;
}
```

vs waitForOutput — identical loop, differing only in `break`+return buffer vs `return true/false`. True duplication (~15 lines). The doc even says "Same stability rule as [waitForOutput]". If the stability rule changes (e.g. turns or deadline semantics), two places to update. SUGGESTION: extract the shared loop.

Another subtle behavior: in `liveWaitForScreen` with settle, each poll iteration that matches re-runs `waitForRawSettle` — with the 2s grace cap. If the CLI stalls mid-frame and NEVER finishes within 2s of the match... then settled screen may still lack item rows; pattern check fails; loop continues; next match re-settles. Total time bounded by `timeout` (15s here). Good.

Edge: `waitForRawSettle` grace of 2s with the deadline checked at loop top; a `settleMs: 200` delay can overshoot deadline by 200ms max. Fine.

One more potential issue: `liveWaitForScreen` timeout accounting — `waitForRawSettle` runs INSIDE the loop without checking the deadline afterwards; total wait can exceed `timeout` by up to ~2s. Minor, documented as "bounded by a grace so ... cannot hang the wait past legacy semantics" — hmm, actually it CAN extend past the timeout by up to 2s per final iteration. That's acceptable/bounded. Not worth more than folding into the duplication comment. Actually — I'll leave it; it's fine, bounded, and the failure mode (slightly longer wait before TimeoutException) is harmless in a 15s visual test.

Now — the fa_cli_visual_test.dart change: after `liveWaitForScreen(..., settle: true)`, assertions read `harness.screenText` rather than the returned settled screen. Between the settle-return and the two `expect` calls, `screenText` is re-read twice. If the pattern's rows scrolled (e.g. fold-hint repaint), the assert could read a different screen than the settled one that satisfied the wait. Practically negligible since the stream just went quiet for 400ms+... but the whole POINT of the returned value is to pin the settled frame. Using the return value would be strictly tighter. SUGGESTION.

